    "def-hh-finite-dual-of-an-associative-algebra": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $k$ be a field ([[def-field]]) and let $A$ be a unital associative $k$-algebra ([[def-algebra-over-a-commutative-ring]]). Write $A^*=\\operatorname{Hom}_k(A,k)$ for its algebraic dual ([[def-algebraic-dual-and-linear-functional]]), and use the $k$-tensor product ([[def-hh-scalar-and-tensor-conventions]]). Its **finite dual** (also called its restricted dual) is the subset\n$$\nA^\\circ:=\\{f\\in A^*: \\text{there is a two-sided ideal }I\\trianglelefteq A \\text{ with }\\dim_k(A/I)<\\infty\\text{ and }f(I)=0\\}.\n$$\nEquivalently, $f\\in A^\\circ$ exactly when it factors through a finite-dimensional quotient algebra $A/I$. The theorem [[thm-hh-finite-dual-coalgebra-and-hopf-descent]] proves that this subset is a vector subspace and that the transposed multiplication $m_A^*(f)$, defined by $m_A^*(f)(a\\otimes b)=f(ab)$, corestricts to $A^\\circ\\otimes A^\\circ$. Until that well-definedness proof is supplied, $m_A^*|_{A^\\circ}$ is only a candidate coproduct."
    ],
    "def-hh-hopf-ideal-and-hopf-subalgebra": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $k$ be a field and let $H=(H,m,u,\\Delta,\\varepsilon,S)$ be a Hopf $k$-algebra as in [[def-hh-hopf-algebra-and-antipode]], with $S$ satisfying both convolution antipode equations. A **subbialgebra** of $H$ is a $k$-subspace $K\\subseteq H$ equipped with a bialgebra structure as in [[def-hh-bialgebra-and-bialgebra-map]] for which the inclusion $K\\hookrightarrow H$ is a bialgebra map. A **Hopf subalgebra** is a subbialgebra $K$ with $S(K)\\subseteq K$. Under AC, meaning every family of nonempty sets has a choice function ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]), the tensor-injection lemma [[lem-hh-tensor-injections-quotients-and-kernels-over-a-field]] identifies $K\\otimes K$ with its image in $H\\otimes H$; in that shorthand, the subbialgebra condition says exactly that $K$ is a unital subalgebra, $\\Delta_H(K)\\subseteq K\\otimes K$, and the counit is restricted from $H$.\n\nA **Hopf ideal** is a two-sided ideal in the sense of [[def-left-right-and-two-sided-ideal]], $I\\trianglelefteq H$, satisfying $\\varepsilon_H(I)=0$, $S(I)\\subseteq I$, and the coideal condition from [[def-hh-subcoalgebras-coideals-and-quotient-coalgebras]]: $\\Delta_H(I)\\subseteq\\operatorname{im}(I\\otimes H\\to H\\otimes H)+\\operatorname{im}(H\\otimes I\\to H\\otimes H)$. Under AC, the tensor-injection lemma identifies these tensor products with subspaces of $H\\otimes H$, giving the usual shorthand $\\Delta_H(I)\\subseteq I\\otimes H+H\\otimes I$. The two-sided ideal condition gives the unital quotient algebra $H/I$; $\\varepsilon_H(I)=0$ makes its counit descend, and the remaining conditions are precisely those needed for the coproduct and antipode to descend. A subbialgebra is not called a Hopf subalgebra unless the antipode restricts to it."
    ],
    "thm-hh-finite-dimensional-dual-hopf-algebra": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $k$ be a field and $H=(H,m,u,\\Delta,\\varepsilon,S)$ a finite-dimensional Hopf $k$-algebra. Give $H^*=\\operatorname{Hom}_k(H,k)$ the transposed Hopf structure: multiplication is convolution,\n$$\n(\\phi\\psi)(h)=(\\phi\\otimes\\psi)(\\Delta(h)),\n$$\nthe unit is $\\varepsilon$, coproduct is the transpose $m^*:H^*\\to H^*\\otimes H^*$ under finite tensor duality, the counit is $\\phi\\mapsto\\phi(1_H)$, and the antipode is $S^*(\\phi)=\\phi\\circ S$. Then $H^*$ is a Hopf algebra. No bijectivity assumption on $S$ is required."
    ],
    "lem-hh-taft-algebra-pbw-and-hopf-structure": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $k$ be a field, $N>1$, and $\\zeta\\in k$ a primitive $N$-th root of unity, with $\\operatorname{char}(k)\\nmid N$. On the $N^2$-dimensional vector space with basis $g^i x^j$, $0\\le i,j<N$, define multiplication on basis elements by $(g^i x^j)(g^a x^b)=\\zeta^{-aj}g^{\\,i+a\\ {\\rm mod}\\ N}x^{j+b}$ if $j+b<N$, and by $0$ if $j+b\\ge N$. Then this is an associative unital algebra and is the presented algebra $k\\langle g,x\\rangle/(g^N-1,\\ x^N,\\ gx-\\zeta xg)$; the displayed basis is a basis of that quotient. It becomes a Hopf algebra with $\\Delta(g)=g\\otimes g$, $\\Delta(x)=1\\otimes x+x\\otimes g$, $\\varepsilon(g)=1$, $\\varepsilon(x)=0$, $S(g)=g^{-1}$, and $S(x)=-xg^{-1}$."
    ],
    "thm-hh-hopf-quotient-kernel-and-tensor-product-constructions": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $k$ be a field. Let $H$ be a Hopf $k$-algebra and $I\\trianglelefteq H$ a Hopf ideal. The quotient $H/I$ has a unique Hopf structure making the quotient map $q:H\\to H/I$ a Hopf map. Every Hopf map $f:H\\to K$ that kills $I$ factors uniquely through $q$ as a Hopf map. For any Hopf map $f:H\\to K$, its kernel is a Hopf ideal.\n\nFor Hopf $k$-algebras $H,K$, the tensor product $H\\otimes K$ is a Hopf algebra with multiplication $m_{H\\otimes K}=(m_H\\otimes m_K)(\\mathrm{id}_H\\otimes\\tau_{K,H}\\otimes\\mathrm{id}_K)$, coproduct $\\Delta_{H\\otimes K}=(\\mathrm{id}_H\\otimes\\tau_{H,K}\\otimes\\mathrm{id}_K)(\\Delta_H\\otimes\\Delta_K)$, unit $u_H\\otimes u_K$, counit $\\varepsilon_H\\otimes\\varepsilon_K$, and antipode $S_H\\otimes S_K$, where $\\tau$ flips the two middle tensor factors. The quotient and tensor-product constructions are choice-free. AC is assumed for the kernel assertion and is used only for tensor-kernel exactness over arbitrary vector spaces."
    ],
    "thm-hh-finite-dual-coalgebra-and-hopf-descent": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $k$ be a field and $A$ a unital associative $k$-algebra. The finite dual $A^\\circ$ is a vector subspace of $A^*$, and $m_A^*(f)$ lies in $A^\\circ\\otimes A^\\circ$ for every $f\\in A^\\circ$. With $\\Delta^\\circ(f)=m_A^*(f)$ and $\\varepsilon^\\circ(f)=f(1_A)$, it is a coalgebra.\n\nMoreover, $f\\in A^\\circ$ exactly when $f$ is a matrix coefficient of a finite-dimensional left $A$-module. If $H$ is a bialgebra, $H^\\circ$ is a bialgebra with convolution product, unit $\\varepsilon_H$, coproduct $m_H^*$, and counit $f\\mapsto f(1_H)$. If $H$ is a Hopf algebra, $H^\\circ$ is closed under $S_H^*(f)=f\\circ S_H$, and this map is an antipode on $H^\\circ$. No finite-dimensionality or bijectivity of $S_H$ is assumed in the last two conclusions."
    ],
